WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.780
Starship's orbital debut plays 26 starlink v3 satellites, each adding roughly 10 times the capacity of a falcon 9 launch. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 120. SpaceX released the first onboard image, showing the deployment sequence from one of the new satellites. 26 v3 satellites reached orbit 25 minutes after liftoff from starbase. Those satellites are expected to add 26 terabytes per second total capacity.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:33.540
roughly 10 times the output of 1 Falcon 9 V2 mini-launch.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:42.860
Ship 41 lost one Raptor vacuum engine early in a send and extended the burn on the remaining 5 engines to reach a safe suborbital path.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:49.700
The booster relit 31 engines for boost back and 11 for the landing burn before splash down in the Gulf.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.940
All 26 satellites established contact through laser links, ground stations, and user terminals.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.420
details. According to the report from StarLust.org, the image came from one of the satellites'

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:06.660
own cameras. Starlink's network supplied live orbital imagery throughout the flight.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:13.260
Three V3 satellites carried dedicated cameras to image the upper-stage heat shield after deployment.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:20.920
Each V3 satellite masses approximately 2,000 kilograms. The design targets one terabit per second

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.920
in downlink and 160 gigabits per second uplink per satellite.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.900
600 gigabits per second inter-satellite lasers

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.660
form the mesh routing backbone.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.040
The Memphis City Council will hold its final vote

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.600
on a data center development moratorium on October 6th.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.220
The pause would allow time to study water

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.300
and energy impacts before approving

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.980
additional hyperscale facilities.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.920
EXAI's Colossus Campus has drawn an average

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.760
of 812,502 gallons of water daily from the Memphis sand aquifer.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:59.280
A rally organized by Indivisible Memphis is scheduled for 3pm ahead of the meeting.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.080
The Beta Communities program lets property owners install one Starlink kit and sell hour,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.980
day, week, or month passes to nearby users.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:14.100
Hosts handle placement, power, and basic upkeep while Starlink manages accounts, billing,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.320
and the satellite link.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:20.780
Passes cover one device for shorter periods and up to four devices for monthly access.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.760
Applications are open in multiple countries.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.240
A Falcon 9 is scheduled to lift off from Vandenberg Space Force Base carrying reconnaissance

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:29.760
satellites.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.240
Viewing sites include local beaches and designated public areas along the central coast.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.800
The launch window opens later today.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:44.080
Amazon has allocated $1 billion toward community outreach and mitigation efforts around proposed

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.840
data center sites. Local opposition groups continue to cite noise, water use, and emissions

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:59.960
concerns in multiple states. SPCX closed at $158.96 after clearing the $158.00 resistance level.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.060
The stock has risen 51.6% from its yearly low. Two additional lockup

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.440
explorations are scheduled for October 7th and October 26th.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.860
Observers at Starbase noted the bronze Prometheus statue erected 10 miles from the pad the

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.540
night before the most recent launch.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.160
The 50-foot sculpture was transported from France by a group of sculptors.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.900
Local hunters in Vermillion Parish, Louisiana expressed mixed views on the plan's Starbase

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.480
Louisiana site, citing both economic hopes and ecosystem concerns.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.500
A resident near White Lake said he plans to watch future launches from his duck blind

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.940
once the new pads open.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.920
NASA astronaut Jessica Mir posted new Earth imagery captured from the crew dragon currently

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:45.960
docked at the station.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.800
The photos show cloud formations and coastlines under varying lighting conditions.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:56.360
One thing worth watching is the Memphis City Council vote on the data center moratorium scheduled

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:57.660
for October 6th.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:02.460
The measure would pause new approvals while the city's studies aquifer drawdown and

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:03.200
power demand.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:08.040
Residents and environmental groups argue the pause is needed to set permanent zoning

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:09.720
rules before further expansion.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.460
On the AI front, XAI's Colossus campus in Memphis has become a focal point for local water

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:18.600
use concerns ahead of the council vote.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:24.940
Amazon's separate $1 billion outreach program shows how hyperscale operators are responding

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.120
running to similar community pushback elsewhere.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:32.940
The Memphis moratorium, if passed, would give regulators time to define specific rules

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.920
for high-density computing facilities.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.740
The engineering angle on the V3 satellites centers on trading mass for bandwidth density.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:47.200
Each satellite masses roughly 2,000 kilograms, more than double earlier versions, yet delivers

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.080
one terabyte per second downlink and 160 gigabits per second uplink.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:05:01.500
That 10-fold capacity jump per satellite is achieved through 600 gigabits per second laser terminals and larger phased array antennas.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:11.360
If raw silicon, solar cells, and propellant were the only costs, the marginal expense per satellite would drop sharply once Starship reaches routine flight rates.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:18.720
The current gap between that raw material floor and the finished satellite price is the classic idiot index for space hardware.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.500
Reuse of the Starship upper stage and on-orbit laser calibration are the two levers expected

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.280
to close it.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.380
The three camera equipped satellites already returning heat shield imagery close an earlier

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:34.700
data loop that previously required either ground tracking or post-flight recovery.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.880
With 26 terabits per second added from a single launch, the constellation economics shift from

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.240
adding many small satellites to fewer higher output nodes.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:50.060
A quick look at the tape shows SNPCX finished the week higher after clearing resistance.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:55.260
Before we go, keep an eye on the Vandenberg launch later today for the first public views

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.840
of the new reconnaissance payload.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.880
And that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments.

